Von Willebrand Disease
Haematology & Oncology
Lifelong easy bruising, Prolonged recurrent epistaxis, Menorrhagia/bleeding post-dental extraction, Reduced vWF antigen and ristocetin cofactor activity

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HMO-050
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, and prolonged bleeding following a minor dental extraction earlier today, completely failing conservative home management.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient who underwent a routine dental extraction 8 hours ago and has been bleeding heavily and continuously from the socket since, filling multiple cups with blood. Urgent in-person evaluation was clinically mandated to achieve direct mechanical haemostasis and evaluate for an underlying severe bleeding diathesis, which could not safely wait until the morning without causing significant hypovolaemia.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I had a tooth pulled this morning, and the hole has been pouring blood constantly for eight hours no matter how hard I bite down on the gauze."
History of Present Illness: The 25-year-old patient reports a significantly prolonged, uncontrollable bleeding episode following a routine molar extraction performed at 10:00 AM. They have utilized multiple gauze packs with continuous pressure, but the socket immediately begins bleeding profusely upon release. Further questioning reveals a lifelong history of easy, extensive superficial bruising (ecchymoses) following absolutely minimal trauma. They endorse recurrent, severe, and prolonged epistaxis lasting greater than 10 minutes throughout childhood, and heavy, prolonged menstrual bleeding (menorrhagia) leading to chronic iron deficiency anaemia. They explicitly note frequent bleeding occurring from the gums during routine aggressive tooth brushing.
Functional Status: Sitting over a sink, actively spitting large volumes of fresh blood. Feeling lightheaded.
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety regarding the continuous blood loss and developing orthostatic symptoms.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 105 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99 percent | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Anxious, pale adult. Oral cavity is filled with fresh, bright red blood.
Physical Exam:
HEENT: Examination of the right lower mandible reveals an actively bleeding molar extraction socket. A large, fragile, and poorly formed "liver clot" (a soft, jelly-like mass of unretracted blood) is extruding from the socket. Continuous, brisk venous oozing occurs around the clot. Normal conjunctivae.
Skin: Multiple superficial, painless ecchymoses in various stages of healing are visible on the bilateral thighs and upper arms. Complete absence of petechiae.
Musculoskeletal: Full, painless range of motion in all major joints. Complete absence of spontaneous deep haemarthroses or muscle haematomas.
Cardiovascular: Tachycardic, regular rhythm. Capillary refill is 2 seconds.
Environmental Observations: Multiple blood-soaked gauze pads and a basin containing approximately 150 mL of fresh blood.
Medication Reconciliation: Took ibuprofen 400mg for dental pain 6 hours ago, which likely severely exacerbated the bleeding tendency.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Von Willebrand Disease - Status: Exacerbated (Acute Post-Surgical Haemorrhage)
Differentials:

Severe Haemophilia A/B - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the complete absence of spontaneous deep joint bleeds (haemarthroses) or massive muscle haematomas, and the prominent history of mucosal/superficial bleeding (menorrhagia, epistaxis), which is the classic hallmark of vWD rather than a primary factor deficiency.

Immune Thrombocytopenic Purpura (ITP) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the lifelong, chronic history of bleeding diathesis and autosomal dominant family history, rather than an acute, sudden onset of profound isolated thrombocytopenia with petechiae.

Simple Localized Surgical Trauma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the extreme, 8-hour duration of brisk bleeding from a minor socket combined with the extensive, highly specific lifelong history of widespread mucosal and superficial bleeding.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits the classic clinical profile of Von Willebrand Disease: a lifelong history of mucosal bleeding (epistaxis, menorrhagia) and easy bruising, culminating in a severe, prolonged haemorrhage following a minor mucosal surgical challenge (dental extraction). The recent ingestion of ibuprofen (an NSAID) further impaired their already defective platelet adhesion, precipitating the current crisis. Delaying assessment until normal hours would expose the patient to continuous, frightening blood loss, progressing to symptomatic hypovolaemia and syncope. The urgent after-hours visit was essential to physically remove the ineffective clot, achieve direct mechanical and chemical haemostasis, and identify the underlying undiagnosed bleeding disorder.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: The large, ineffective "liver clot" was manually removed from the socket to allow access to the bleeding source. Firm, continuous pressure was applied using gauze soaked in tranexamic acid (a topical antifibrinolytic), achieving complete haemostasis after 15 minutes. Arranged an urgent outpatient haematology referral for specific diagnostic testing (anticipating functionally decreased ristocetin cofactor activity and reduced vWF antigen).
Positive Behavioral Change:

Bite down constantly and firmly on the medicated gauze for the next two hours without talking or opening your mouth to check the wound. By maintaining absolute, uninterrupted mechanical pressure, you force the blood vessels to collapse, allowing the chemical medication to build a solid, permanent plug in the socket.

Completely cease taking ibuprofen, aspirin, or any other NSAIDs for pain, using only paracetamol. By avoiding these specific drugs, you prevent chemically disabling the few functioning platelets you have, directly stopping future uncontrollable bleeding episodes.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days to arrange the formal haematology blood panel.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present immediately to the Emergency Department if the bleeding restarts as a continuous, brisk flow that fills your mouth entirely within minutes and completely fails to stop after thirty minutes of biting down hard on fresh, dry gauze.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Your lifelong history of bruising and this severe bleeding strongly suggest an inherited disorder where your blood lacks a specific sticking protein; specialized laboratory tests are required to confirm this.
Clinical Course: The medication applied to the socket prevents the blood clot from dissolving prematurely. Once the bleeding is permanently stopped, you will require a specialist plan to manage any future surgeries or dental work safely.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not rinse your mouth, spit forcefully, or drink through a straw for the next 24 hours. The suction will instantly pull the new clot out of the socket.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your blood is likely missing 'Von Willebrand factor', which acts like the glue holding platelets to the wall of a broken blood vessel. Taking the ibuprofen completely destroyed what little sticking power your platelets had, causing the endless bleeding. The medicated gauze we applied actively prevents your saliva from breaking down the fragile new clot while it hardens.
Actionable Steps: Bite down firmly. Never take ibuprofen again. Do not spit or rinse. Follow up for formal blood testing.

